WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.529 --> 00:00:06.950
Ben Wattenberg: Hello, I’m Ben Wattenberg.

00:00:06.950 --> 00:00:12.240
There is a great deal of sex and violence
on television, in the movies, and in our popular

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:13.240
music.

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:16.500
Does this lead to unacceptable behavior?

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:18.780
Is this coarsening our lives?

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:24.759
Or are sex and violence age-old staples of
art and literature?

00:00:24.759 --> 00:00:30.590
In any event, what tools are available in
a free society to change popular culture?

00:00:30.590 --> 00:00:35.980
Joining us to sort through the conflict and
the consensus are Camille Paglia, professor

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:40.380
of humanities at the University of the Arts
in Philadelphia and author of the recently

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:46.769
published “Vamps and Tramps”; Robert Bork,
senior fellow at the American Enterprise Institute

00:00:46.769 --> 00:00:53.249
and author of the forthcoming book, “Slouching
Toward Gomorrah: Modern Liberalism and American

00:00:53.249 --> 00:00:59.969
Decline”; and John Leo, columnist for US
News and World Report and author of “Two

00:00:59.969 --> 00:01:02.149
Steps Ahead of the Thought Police.”

00:01:02.149 --> 00:01:07.060
The topic before this house: Does Hollywood
hurt America?

00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:18.280
This week on “Think Tank.”

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:25.770
It is one of America’s great ironies.

00:01:25.770 --> 00:01:30.370
We love to watch television, to go to the
movies, and to listen to popular music, but

00:01:30.370 --> 00:01:36.070
when we do, many of us strongly object to
what we see or hear, and we are especially

00:01:36.070 --> 00:01:40.119
concerned about what our children see and
hear.

00:01:40.119 --> 00:01:45.920
Recently, Senate majority leader and Republican
presidential contender Bob Dole attacked Hollywood.

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:51.229
Bob Dole [from videotape]: Our music, movies,
television, and advertising regularly push

00:01:51.229 --> 00:01:56.040
the limits of decency, and they bombard our
children with the destructive message of casual

00:01:56.040 --> 00:02:00.109
violence and even more casual sex.

00:02:00.109 --> 00:02:05.189
And I concluded that we must hold Hollywood
and the entire entertainment industry accountable

00:02:05.189 --> 00:02:07.710
for putting profit ahead of common decency.

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:33.819
Ben Wattenberg: Dole criticized movies like
“Natural Born Killers” and “True Romance.”

00:02:33.819 --> 00:02:40.120
Dole and others have singled out the Time
Warner Corporation for particular criticism.

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:42.990
C. Dolores Tucker [from videotape]: I’m
C. Dolores Tucker, chair of the National Political

00:02:42.990 --> 00:02:44.200
Congress of Black Women.

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:46.650
William Bennett [from videotape]: I’m Bill
Bennett, codirector of Empower America.

00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:48.739
C. Dolores Tucker [from videotape]: I’m
a liberal Democrat.

00:02:48.739 --> 00:02:50.590
William Bennett [from videotape]: And I’m
a conservative Republican.

00:02:50.590 --> 00:02:54.480
C. Dolores Tucker [from videotape]: Time Warner’s
music division promotes music that celebrates

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:58.290
the rape, torture, and murder of women.

00:02:58.290 --> 00:03:00.830
The lyrics are both offensive and do terrible
harm to our children.

00:03:00.830 --> 00:03:04.060
William Bennett [from videotape]: Isn’t
anybody at Time Warner embarrassed by these

00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:05.060
lyrics?

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:08.180
Ben Wattenberg: Time Warner’s response has
been quite cautious.

00:03:08.180 --> 00:03:14.960
“It is certainly not an easy matter to decide
who the ultimate arbiter of creative content

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:16.250
should be.

00:03:16.250 --> 00:03:22.269
We prefer to err on the side of freedom of
expression.”

00:03:22.269 --> 00:03:24.380
President Clinton had spoken out even earlier.

00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:27.151
Bill Clinton [from videotape]: People in the
entertainment industry in this country, we

00:03:27.151 --> 00:03:34.129
applaud your creativity and your worldwide
success, and we support your freedom of expression.

00:03:34.129 --> 00:03:38.750
But you do have a responsibility to assess
the impact of your work and to understand

00:03:38.750 --> 00:03:44.780
the damage that comes from the incessant,
repetitive, mindless violence and irresponsible

00:03:44.780 --> 00:03:52.459
conduct that permeates our media all the time.

00:03:52.459 --> 00:03:53.459
(Applause.)

00:03:53.459 --> 00:03:57.209
Ben Wattenberg: Okay, panel, lady and gentlemen,
President Clinton and Senator Dole think that

00:03:57.209 --> 00:04:02.180
sex and violence in the movies can cause bad
behavior.

00:04:02.180 --> 00:04:07.040
Judge Bob Bork, do you think that there is
a relationship between what we see on our

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:10.700
television screens and hear in popular music
and actual behavior?

00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:15.510
Robert Bork: If there isn’t, the advertisers
are wasting billions of dollars a year.

00:04:15.510 --> 00:04:23.139
Michael Medved cites over 3,000 studies that
show that a diet of constant violence in entertainment

00:04:23.139 --> 00:04:26.050
leads to aggressive, antisocial behavior.

00:04:26.050 --> 00:04:30.850
As to the word sex, I think the lady who worked
with the single mothers put it best.

00:04:30.850 --> 00:04:32.450
They said, how can we stop illegitimacy?

00:04:32.450 --> 00:04:34.220
She said, “Shoot Madonna.”

00:04:34.220 --> 00:04:38.300
Now, that may be a little extreme, but you
see her point.

00:04:38.300 --> 00:04:44.810
Ben Wattenberg: Camille Paglia, does depraved
or decadent popular culture cause depraved

00:04:44.810 --> 00:04:45.810
or decadent behavior?

00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:50.200
Camille Paglia: Well, as a great fan of Madonna,
I have to say that I am more skeptical than

00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:56.930
Judge Bork of those thousands of studies,
which I believe only show an anecdotal and

00:04:56.930 --> 00:05:01.320
incidental kind of relationship between what
is seen on screen and what is done on the

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:02.320
street.

00:05:02.320 --> 00:05:08.270
I think in fact that violent fictions may
provide a kind of cathartic release for certain

00:05:08.270 --> 00:05:09.750
eternal human energies.

00:05:09.750 --> 00:05:13.509
I mean, by this standard, we would have to
look very closely at “Hamlet,” let’s

00:05:13.509 --> 00:05:17.210
say, or — which has multiple corpses on
the stage at the end.

00:05:17.210 --> 00:05:20.960
This was, in effect, the argument that the
Puritans used to close the theaters in Shakespeare’s

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:21.960
time.

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:22.960
Or we’d have to look at “Oedipus Rex.”

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:26.150
All of Greek drama, it seems to me, is full
of barbarities.

00:05:26.150 --> 00:05:27.520
Ben Wattenberg: Okay.

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:28.520
John Leo.

00:05:28.520 --> 00:05:29.970
John Leo: I think it obviously does have an
effect.

00:05:29.970 --> 00:05:34.110
I mean the popular culture is the air that
our kids breathe.

00:05:34.110 --> 00:05:38.530
And if you call women whores and bitches in
a thousand songs, you shouldn’t be surprised

00:05:38.530 --> 00:05:40.479
if young males start to treat them that way.

00:05:40.479 --> 00:05:45.460
Ben Wattenberg: How do you account for the
fact — I mean Camille alluded to it — that

00:05:45.460 --> 00:05:52.270
in earlier times, I mean we had cartoons and
Westerns and private-eye movies and monster

00:05:52.270 --> 00:05:58.410
movies and cop shows, as well as terrible
violence in Shakespeare and Sophocles and

00:05:58.410 --> 00:06:02.949
terrible — not terrible — interesting,
amusing sexuality in Aristophanes and so on

00:06:02.949 --> 00:06:03.949
and so forth.

00:06:03.949 --> 00:06:08.360
So why do you claim that now is different,
either of you?

00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:09.360
John Leo: Let me jump in.

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:13.760
I think that Camille is right that there is
a cathartic aspect to it, but that works best

00:06:13.760 --> 00:06:16.419
in a stable culture with social controls.

00:06:16.419 --> 00:06:20.760
When you have a culture that seems to be melting
down, as our does, you are going to find the

00:06:20.760 --> 00:06:25.690
next generation taking its messages of quick
solutions through violence from the screen.

00:06:25.690 --> 00:06:27.870
It’s not just that there is some violence
in “Hamlet.”

00:06:27.870 --> 00:06:33.000
It’s that our children are watching thousands
of murders and Bruce Willis is upping the

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:35.470
body count in every “Diehard.”

00:06:35.470 --> 00:06:38.990
I think that gets through to kids, particularly
young males, that that’s the way to solve

00:06:38.990 --> 00:06:41.000
things and it’s an OK solution.

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:45.000
Robert Bork: Kids watch about 20 hours of
television a week.

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:47.760
And the count for violence and murder is very
high.

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:50.729
I forget exactly what it is, but I think that
they see nine murders an evening.

00:06:50.729 --> 00:06:53.470
They don’t see Shakespeare that many times.

00:06:53.470 --> 00:06:54.680
We’re drenched in violence.

00:06:54.680 --> 00:07:00.009
Ben Wattenberg: No, but people in Shakespeare’s
time saw Shakespeare that many times, or Sophocles

00:07:00.009 --> 00:07:01.009
in Sophocles’ —

00:07:01.009 --> 00:07:03.669
Robert Bork: I don’t think anybody saw Shakespeare
20 hours a week.

00:07:03.669 --> 00:07:07.550
But be that as it may, also the violence was
presented in a different context there.

00:07:07.550 --> 00:07:10.830
It had a plot to it, and there was a reason
for it.

00:07:10.830 --> 00:07:13.960
The violence now, they always refer to it
as senseless violence.

00:07:13.960 --> 00:07:14.960
It is.

00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:17.389
It’s just for the pleasure of seeing bodies
fly apart.

00:07:17.389 --> 00:07:20.669
Camille Paglia: Yeah, I think there has been
a terrible decline in aesthetic standards

00:07:20.669 --> 00:07:26.080
in Hollywood, but I feel this is due to the
transition from the great Hollywood studio

00:07:26.080 --> 00:07:32.830
era to the America of the 1950s, when television
has now kept mass audiences at home.

00:07:32.830 --> 00:07:37.759
So over the last 30 years, I feel, there has
been an evolution in the kind of movies being

00:07:37.759 --> 00:07:38.759
made.

00:07:38.759 --> 00:07:42.500
The only people who really go out to movies
now are young people, people on dates, who

00:07:42.500 --> 00:07:47.460
like these kinds of cartoony mutilation of
bodies, and so on.

00:07:47.460 --> 00:07:53.629
There’s no doubt that just in the period
since I was in college, this was a great era

00:07:53.629 --> 00:07:58.330
of incredible classy, philosophical foreign
films, art films that are completely gone

00:07:58.330 --> 00:07:59.330
now.

00:07:59.330 --> 00:08:03.940
I do — I am concerned as a professor about
the condition of America.

00:08:03.940 --> 00:08:08.530
I’m a great lover of popular culture, but
we cannot have the young raised only on a

00:08:08.530 --> 00:08:09.669
diet of popular culture.

00:08:09.669 --> 00:08:14.900
This is why I am for also the canon of great
writers in order to balance out this.

00:08:14.900 --> 00:08:21.639
So as opposed to censorship, I say build up
the high art and the study of great thinkers.

00:08:21.639 --> 00:08:26.970
Ben Wattenberg: But people other than teenagers,
including me, do watch movies.

00:08:26.970 --> 00:08:30.970
I happen to think movies — I mean the ones
I watch — are pretty wonderful.

00:08:30.970 --> 00:08:33.920
We have a — it is a great American art form.

00:08:33.920 --> 00:08:38.430
It is being watched by more people these days,
plus the international market.

00:08:38.430 --> 00:08:42.040
This is what is showing America’s face,
for good or for ill.

00:08:42.040 --> 00:08:43.400
I think mostly for good.

00:08:43.400 --> 00:08:49.860
I think many of those movies have a message
of American individualism and upward mobility.

00:08:49.860 --> 00:08:53.810
And so why are we — why are we beating up
on the fact that some of it is rotten?

00:08:53.810 --> 00:08:58.070
John Leo: Well, let me give you an example,
I’ll use a Republican perpetrator, since

00:08:58.070 --> 00:08:59.480
Bob Dole didn’t.

00:08:59.480 --> 00:09:04.060
I like the “Terminator” movies, but in
“Terminator 2,” Schwarzenegger comes down

00:09:04.060 --> 00:09:08.620
naked from the future, and his first act is
to walk into a pool room and beat the living

00:09:08.620 --> 00:09:11.790
hell out of these guys who have jackets and
motorcycles that he wants.

00:09:11.790 --> 00:09:16.170
Well, first of all, this is a typical urban
crime, but secondly, it’s also seen through

00:09:16.170 --> 00:09:17.170
his eyes.

00:09:17.170 --> 00:09:21.380
You walk in, you see it through his eyes,
and you’re invited to enjoy the decimation

00:09:21.380 --> 00:09:23.130
of these characters — who have done nothing.

00:09:23.130 --> 00:09:28.890
It’s a pro-violence message in a way that
you didn’t used to get in American movies.

00:09:28.890 --> 00:09:33.410
You are given the taste and feel of violence,
and it’s made very inviting.

00:09:33.410 --> 00:09:34.410
And you’re justified.

00:09:34.410 --> 00:09:36.030
You’re here to stop World War III, so it’s
OK.

00:09:36.030 --> 00:09:38.750
Camille Paglia: But again, I don’t feel
that there’s a direct relationship between

00:09:38.750 --> 00:09:42.140
such movies and such scenes and the actual
violence in the streets.

00:09:42.140 --> 00:09:46.460
For me, there are sociological factors for
the violence in the streets, and that is the

00:09:46.460 --> 00:09:52.070
utter breakdown of community life in the inner
cities because of the flight of the black

00:09:52.070 --> 00:09:56.640
middle class to the suburbs and the absence
now of manufacturing jobs.

00:09:56.640 --> 00:09:59.020
There are a lot of unemployed black kids.

00:09:59.020 --> 00:10:04.700
I, in point of fact, can remember stories
that my relatives told of the violence of

00:10:04.700 --> 00:10:08.590
Italian-American youth 60 years ago, let’s
say.

00:10:08.590 --> 00:10:15.130
We should really examine, you know, the level
of juvenile delinquency among ethnics other

00:10:15.130 --> 00:10:17.440
than blacks from the early part of the century.

00:10:17.440 --> 00:10:21.570
I’m not so sure — now they have better
weapons than they did then.

00:10:21.570 --> 00:10:25.670
They used to settle problems with knives and
fists, and now, unfortunately, with Uzis.

00:10:25.670 --> 00:10:29.580
Ben Wattenberg: Haven’t you written, Camille,
that you think pornography is good stuff?

00:10:29.580 --> 00:10:30.580
Camille Paglia: Yes.

00:10:30.580 --> 00:10:35.370
I am radically pro-porn — I know that that
is an extreme position — because I feel

00:10:35.370 --> 00:10:39.980
that pornography shows the truth about sexuality,
the truth about our animal natures.

00:10:39.980 --> 00:10:45.200
At the same time, I feel that it is appropriate
to ask that pornography not be displayed in

00:10:45.200 --> 00:10:50.120
public spaces, that it should be available
for private consumption, that no one has the

00:10:50.120 --> 00:10:53.000
right to intrude into public spaces and to
—

00:10:53.000 --> 00:10:55.020
Ben Wattenberg: Is a movie theater a public
space?

00:10:55.020 --> 00:11:01.120
Camille Paglia: No, not in the sense that
a public square or, let’s say, even newsstand

00:11:01.120 --> 00:11:02.120
would be.

00:11:02.120 --> 00:11:04.060
In other words, I want the pornography available
at the newsstand.

00:11:04.060 --> 00:11:08.100
I don’t feel anyone has the right to display
the pornography for Christian people who are

00:11:08.100 --> 00:11:09.100
wandering on the street.

00:11:09.100 --> 00:11:11.800
Robert Bork: Well, the problem is that the
people who enjoy the pornography change.

00:11:11.800 --> 00:11:16.830
They change themselves, and they change themselves
for the worse.

00:11:16.830 --> 00:11:23.640
On internet right now, I’ve learned that
you can get alt sex stories, and there you

00:11:23.640 --> 00:11:29.270
can read about the castration of a 7-year-old
boy that is being shot, about the gang rape

00:11:29.270 --> 00:11:30.980
of a 6-year-old girl.

00:11:30.980 --> 00:11:35.030
You can get stories telling you when to lurk
outside a girl’s school, how to bundle into

00:11:35.030 --> 00:11:39.200
the van, whether to tell her in advance that
after you’re finished with her, she’s

00:11:39.200 --> 00:11:40.400
going to be murdered.

00:11:40.400 --> 00:11:42.840
That stuff is on the internet now.

00:11:42.840 --> 00:11:45.970
Wait till George Gilder’s digital films
from all over the world are available.

00:11:45.970 --> 00:11:48.130
Ben Wattenberg: And you have just repeated
it on public television.

00:11:48.130 --> 00:11:50.090
Robert Bork: Not in the same loving detail.

00:11:50.090 --> 00:11:55.950
Camille Paglia: Let me say about that, this
proves my point that the effort, the coalition

00:11:55.950 --> 00:12:02.330
between the religious right and the feminist
anti-porn wing has failed, that they thought

00:12:02.330 --> 00:12:08.390
they had finally cornered the market and limited
it in the realm of pornographic films and

00:12:08.390 --> 00:12:09.390
videos.

00:12:09.390 --> 00:12:13.839
But look, the moment there’s a new medium
of communication, the — what can I say?

00:12:13.839 --> 00:12:18.100
— the perverse human soul bubbles up everywhere.

00:12:18.100 --> 00:12:24.360
And so to me, the presence of these horrific
stories on the internet simply proves my point,

00:12:24.360 --> 00:12:27.660
that it may be the human imagination that
we cannot fully police.

00:12:27.660 --> 00:12:31.060
Robert Bork: Well, we used to have restraints
of various kinds, not only law, but religion

00:12:31.060 --> 00:12:35.130
and morality, that kept these things in the
corner at best.

00:12:35.130 --> 00:12:39.980
Now it’s out in the open, and it may be
the technology is such that it will be impossible

00:12:39.980 --> 00:12:40.980
to control it.

00:12:40.980 --> 00:12:42.340
Ben Wattenberg: All right, well, let — go
ahead, John.

00:12:42.340 --> 00:12:46.650
John Leo: Well, I was going to say that I
agree with Camille that various ethnic groups

00:12:46.650 --> 00:12:47.890
commit horrific violence.

00:12:47.890 --> 00:12:52.570
The Irish were very good at it into the last
century, but it usually came under control

00:12:52.570 --> 00:12:56.290
because of the churches and the institutions
which are now in collapse.

00:12:56.290 --> 00:13:02.090
So I think the attention to the public culture
now is partly to acknowledge that the family

00:13:02.090 --> 00:13:07.470
and the churches can’t stop this, that the
public culture is the preacher now, so whatever

00:13:07.470 --> 00:13:11.500
is put into the pot of the public culture
is likely to have direct effects that it didn’t

00:13:11.500 --> 00:13:12.500
have before.

00:13:12.500 --> 00:13:16.010
Ben Wattenberg: All right, let us stipulate
that.

00:13:16.010 --> 00:13:24.540
You have at least one skeptic here, me, but
let us stipulate that bad culture drives bad

00:13:24.540 --> 00:13:26.330
behavior.

00:13:26.330 --> 00:13:29.120
What are you going to do about it?

00:13:29.120 --> 00:13:32.100
You alluded fondly to censorship.

00:13:32.100 --> 00:13:33.730
Robert Bork: Fondly — well, yes.

00:13:33.730 --> 00:13:37.440
I think it’s inevitable that we’re going
to try censorship at some stage.

00:13:37.440 --> 00:13:38.900
I didn’t used to think so.

00:13:38.900 --> 00:13:43.240
It hought that the public culture was beyond
agreement on any form of censorship.

00:13:43.240 --> 00:13:48.060
But when you get the kind of stuff that’s
now on the internet, the kind of stuff I described,

00:13:48.060 --> 00:13:52.021
and when that appears in digital films you
can watch on your home computer, I think we’re

00:13:52.021 --> 00:13:55.990
in a very dangerous situation as we begin
to appeal to perverts who could be triggered

00:13:55.990 --> 00:13:57.010
into action.

00:13:57.010 --> 00:13:59.140
I think we’re going to have to try censorship.

00:13:59.140 --> 00:14:00.620
Whether we can do it or not, I don’t know.

00:14:00.620 --> 00:14:02.120
Ben Wattenberg: You would like to see it succeed?

00:14:02.120 --> 00:14:03.120
Robert Bork: Oh, yes.

00:14:03.120 --> 00:14:06.990
I don’t want that kind of stuff floating
around in this society.

00:14:06.990 --> 00:14:13.760
Ben Wattenberg: Any — I mean not even undisplayed
at a public newsstand, the way Camille was

00:14:13.760 --> 00:14:14.760
saying?

00:14:14.760 --> 00:14:18.290
It’s not out in front of you, but you can
say, I want a copy of “Hustler” and get

00:14:18.290 --> 00:14:19.290
it.

00:14:19.290 --> 00:14:22.140
Robert Bork: I’m worried about the fellow
who wants to get stories about young children

00:14:22.140 --> 00:14:24.170
being raped and murdered.

00:14:24.170 --> 00:14:27.390
That is not a normal mind, and I don’t want
to trigger that mind.

00:14:27.390 --> 00:14:31.760
Ben Wattenberg: What about “Playboy,”
“Penthouse,” getting down to “Hustler”?

00:14:31.760 --> 00:14:32.760
What about those magazines?

00:14:32.760 --> 00:14:36.310
Robert Bork: Oh, I haven’t seen — I’m
too embarrassed in the barbershop to pick

00:14:36.310 --> 00:14:38.673
one up, so I haven’t really seen one for
a long time.

00:14:38.673 --> 00:14:40.930
Ben Wattenberg: As part of your research,
you could purchase one.

00:14:40.930 --> 00:14:42.660
Robert Bork: Yeah, that’s right.

00:14:42.660 --> 00:14:43.660
(Laughter.)

00:14:43.660 --> 00:14:47.515
No, I have seen — occasionally seen them,
and some of them, I think, deserve censorship.

00:14:47.515 --> 00:14:48.990
John Leo: I think the real problem is —

00:14:48.990 --> 00:14:51.680
Ben Wattenberg: John, you’re in the First
Amendment business.

00:14:51.680 --> 00:14:52.700
John Leo: Sure.

00:14:52.700 --> 00:14:57.880
No, I’m not for censorship, but my solution,
however dreamy, is a change in consciousness.

00:14:57.880 --> 00:15:02.790
I think cultures do change, sexual attitudes
change abruptly from generation to generation,

00:15:02.790 --> 00:15:05.760
as they have through English history quite
regularly.

00:15:05.760 --> 00:15:07.870
And I think we can change consciousness in
America.

00:15:07.870 --> 00:15:08.870
Ben Wattenberg: Camille.

00:15:08.870 --> 00:15:13.990
Camille Paglia: Well, my analysis of pornography
and history has been that the more there is

00:15:13.990 --> 00:15:20.290
a taboo of any kind in a culture, the more
you will get imaginations that profane that

00:15:20.290 --> 00:15:21.890
sacred subject, whatever it is.

00:15:21.890 --> 00:15:28.020
So I have the feeling that possibly the majority
of these horrific fantasies of child abuse

00:15:28.020 --> 00:15:33.140
and murder and so on may be coming from other
forces in the culture, not because people

00:15:33.140 --> 00:15:35.450
want to enact such frightful scenarios.

00:15:35.450 --> 00:15:42.080
So, and I — again, I — as a student of
psychopathology, I am simply doubtful of a

00:15:42.080 --> 00:15:45.890
direct correlation between people who indulge
in such fantasies and the people who actually

00:15:45.890 --> 00:15:50.980
carry them out, because we have the example
of Jules Duree (sp) and the Countess Dracula

00:15:50.980 --> 00:15:51.980
and Nero.

00:15:51.980 --> 00:15:57.640
These, such examples, they did not need printed
pornography or internet in order to stimulate

00:15:57.640 --> 00:15:58.640
their acts.

00:15:58.640 --> 00:15:59.640
John Leo: We don’t know.

00:15:59.640 --> 00:16:00.640
Robert Bork: No, we don’t.

00:16:00.640 --> 00:16:01.640
That’s right.

00:16:01.640 --> 00:16:02.640
Remember those two young boys in England who
killed the toddler?

00:16:02.640 --> 00:16:03.640
Camille Paglia: Mm-hmm.

00:16:03.640 --> 00:16:05.692
Robert Bork: It turns out that their father
was an addict of snuff films, and one of them

00:16:05.692 --> 00:16:08.750
was the precise way in which they killed that
kid.

00:16:08.750 --> 00:16:12.540
And there were some boys who just strangled
a friend from the rear seat of a car — they

00:16:12.540 --> 00:16:14.170
strangled — sitting in the front seat.

00:16:14.170 --> 00:16:15.780
They got that from “Godfather II,” they
said.

00:16:15.780 --> 00:16:20.420
Camille Paglia: But it may be that — millions
and millions of people may see similar scenes;

00:16:20.420 --> 00:16:26.130
only a small number may be pushed into committing
such acts, but perhaps they would have done

00:16:26.130 --> 00:16:27.130
these things anyway.

00:16:27.130 --> 00:16:34.600
Are we going to close down the whole society
in order to prevent such I would say sociopaths

00:16:34.600 --> 00:16:37.030
from acting who might have acted anyway?

00:16:37.030 --> 00:16:42.940
Ben Wattenberg: John, you have written about
the role of shame to get change done.

00:16:42.940 --> 00:16:45.240
Is that what you want to do when you say you
want to change the culture?

00:16:45.240 --> 00:16:50.160
John Leo: That’s why I think the campaign
against Time Warner is so interesting.

00:16:50.160 --> 00:16:53.890
What it is, it’s a clear appeal to shame,
and I think it’s working.

00:16:53.890 --> 00:16:58.660
The noises I hear within the Time Warner empire
are shame noises.

00:16:58.660 --> 00:16:59.910
We can’t make them do anything.

00:16:59.910 --> 00:17:04.199
It’s a tiny part of their empire; they’re
a tiny part of the cultural pollution business,

00:17:04.199 --> 00:17:05.199
but they are writhing.

00:17:05.199 --> 00:17:06.199
They’re stunned by this.

00:17:06.199 --> 00:17:07.559
They don’t know what to do.

00:17:07.559 --> 00:17:12.490
It’s the purest leverage that shame induction
has had in years.

00:17:12.490 --> 00:17:13.700
And I think it’s a good lesson.

00:17:13.700 --> 00:17:16.020
Ben Wattenberg: Yeah, but what is the bottom
line?

00:17:16.020 --> 00:17:20.980
Let us say that your campaign, and it’s
a larger campaign now, to sort of shame Time

00:17:20.980 --> 00:17:25.459
Warner, which is one of the big guys on the
block, to get rid of that sort of gangsta

00:17:25.459 --> 00:17:26.459
rap music —

00:17:26.459 --> 00:17:28.370
John Leo: Someone else picks it up.

00:17:28.370 --> 00:17:33.030
Ben Wattenberg: Someone else picks it up,
let’s say another big company, I guess Sony,

00:17:33.030 --> 00:17:36.440
whatever, and they can be shamed into it — or
shamed out of it.

00:17:36.440 --> 00:17:40.460
But then you go down the food chain to smaller
companies, or if you can shame the smaller

00:17:40.460 --> 00:17:44.290
companies that — and that’s hard, because
they don’t have theme parks, they don’t

00:17:44.290 --> 00:17:47.760
have books, they don’t have a lot of other
things you can boycott, but suppose you can,

00:17:47.760 --> 00:17:51.240
then you’re getting new — brand new companies
putting out gangsta rap.

00:17:51.240 --> 00:17:56.550
John Leo: I don’t see the shame campaign
going company to company, garage to garage.

00:17:56.550 --> 00:18:02.030
I think we’re trying to get a psychic movement
going among people, and so that it really

00:18:02.030 --> 00:18:04.200
is a demand-side revolution.

00:18:04.200 --> 00:18:08.240
You’re going to have to tell people that
we are all ashamed of this stuff and let’s

00:18:08.240 --> 00:18:09.240
not buy it.

00:18:09.240 --> 00:18:12.180
So you’re not going to go company by company.

00:18:12.180 --> 00:18:14.010
It’s not a supply-end thing.

00:18:14.010 --> 00:18:16.970
We’re just trying to get the whole mechanism
going in the culture.

00:18:16.970 --> 00:18:19.700
Robert Bork: Well, what you have to do is
rebuild authority in the family because the

00:18:19.700 --> 00:18:24.990
people who are buying this gangsta rap are
predominantly white suburban adolescents whose

00:18:24.990 --> 00:18:26.560
parents are letting them do it.

00:18:26.560 --> 00:18:30.600
John Leo: The other thing that’s happening
is this is creating a great new debate within

00:18:30.600 --> 00:18:31.600
the black community.

00:18:31.600 --> 00:18:34.520
I think black people are very conflicted about
this.

00:18:34.520 --> 00:18:37.030
They don’t like to see their culture attacked
by whites.

00:18:37.030 --> 00:18:42.200
On the other hand, a lot of them are very
nervous about the image of black people that’s

00:18:42.200 --> 00:18:43.990
being put out in these songs.

00:18:43.990 --> 00:18:49.430
So I think this campaign, and to the great
credit of Dr. Tucker, has given great leverage

00:18:49.430 --> 00:18:51.990
to people in the black community who want
change.

00:18:51.990 --> 00:18:56.490
Camille Paglia: I believe that there should
be public incentives for the great producers

00:18:56.490 --> 00:19:01.550
and the — and Time Warner, and so on — to
start focusing on quality productions.

00:19:01.550 --> 00:19:04.110
And I don’t mean Shirley Temple productions.

00:19:04.110 --> 00:19:09.870
But I think when the momentum of the culture
is to demand aesthetics in the style, again,

00:19:09.870 --> 00:19:14.570
of the old Hollywood studio system, where
— we look back at some of those movies,

00:19:14.570 --> 00:19:20.500
extraordinary quality at every level, from
the lighting to the makeup to narrative to

00:19:20.500 --> 00:19:21.710
plot, and so on.

00:19:21.710 --> 00:19:25.200
And you could sit with grandparents and children
in the same room and not be embarrassed.

00:19:25.200 --> 00:19:28.480
You could have a multigenerational experience
in watching —

00:19:28.480 --> 00:19:32.460
Ben Wattenberg: And that all passed through
the legendary Hayes office that was censorship.

00:19:32.460 --> 00:19:36.520
Camille Paglia: Well, you see, these arguments
actually were going on in early Hollywood.

00:19:36.520 --> 00:19:41.910
There were producers who wanted to make movies
more upscale, to make them — to bring culture

00:19:41.910 --> 00:19:44.890
to the masses — others who wanted to bring
blood and guts.

00:19:44.890 --> 00:19:50.770
After all, let’s remember the great circulation
wars of the William Randolph Hearst era.

00:19:50.770 --> 00:19:55.870
It appears to be the case that the working-class
audiences, the popular audience really does

00:19:55.870 --> 00:20:02.020
like kind of a high level of sex and violence
that may make upper-middle-class white people

00:20:02.020 --> 00:20:03.020
uncomfortable.

00:20:03.020 --> 00:20:09.070
So I worry about a kind of elitist attitude
that perhaps has crept into some of this cultural

00:20:09.070 --> 00:20:10.070
debate.

00:20:10.070 --> 00:20:11.070
Robert Bork: I don’t know that it’s a
class attitude, but there are differences

00:20:11.070 --> 00:20:12.070
among people.

00:20:12.070 --> 00:20:15.760
And there are some people who would be better
off if some of that material is kept out of

00:20:15.760 --> 00:20:16.760
their hands.

00:20:16.760 --> 00:20:21.280
And it was, as Ben said, censorship agreed
to by the industry through the Hayes office.

00:20:21.280 --> 00:20:23.980
And the best movies — that was the golden
era of Hollywood.

00:20:23.980 --> 00:20:25.760
Camille Paglia: The ’30s, yes.

00:20:25.760 --> 00:20:30.370
As it happens, and this is true, after — I
hate to say it because I do love lurid pornography

00:20:30.370 --> 00:20:35.870
— but after the Hayes rules went through,
we had fantastic films of the '30s and'40s

00:20:35.870 --> 00:20:37.300
that are still studied in classrooms.

00:20:37.300 --> 00:20:43.060
Ben Wattenberg: Bob, you had alluded to it
— family values, religious values.

00:20:43.060 --> 00:20:50.880
Are we in for what would be what the fourth
great awakening, a turn of the cultural pendulum?

00:20:50.880 --> 00:20:55.970
Robert Bork: Well, some people see — for
example, this promise-keepers movement, which

00:20:55.970 --> 00:21:00.890
is men getting together, 50,000, 75,000, in
an auditorium where they pay money and they

00:21:00.890 --> 00:21:04.910
come and they pray and they sing hymns and
they promise fidelity to their wives, and

00:21:04.910 --> 00:21:07.720
so forth — some see that as the beginning
of a religious awakening.

00:21:07.720 --> 00:21:13.770
But I really think the public culture could
only be reformed by a religious awakening.

00:21:13.770 --> 00:21:17.930
In the regency in England at the beginning
of the last century, it was a dissolute, corrupt

00:21:17.930 --> 00:21:21.270
culture, and it was followed by the Victorian
era.

00:21:21.270 --> 00:21:24.540
And the difference, apparently, was Methodism
percolating through the upper classes and

00:21:24.540 --> 00:21:27.930
upper-middle classes, and then to the working
classes.

00:21:27.930 --> 00:21:30.590
So without a religious revival, I’m not
sure where we’re going.

00:21:30.590 --> 00:21:35.060
Camille Paglia: I have to say that I agree
with what you just said, Judge Bork.

00:21:35.060 --> 00:21:37.630
I speak as a radical ’60s libertarian.

00:21:37.630 --> 00:21:41.900
I think, in fact, that there is a movement
back toward religious fundamentalism in the

00:21:41.900 --> 00:21:46.650
world because of the failure of modernism,
modernism which gave only nihilism and has

00:21:46.650 --> 00:21:50.260
destroyed the education, I think, of — you
know, for the masses.

00:21:50.260 --> 00:21:55.640
I’ve been warning my fellow liberals for
a long time that they have pushed the country

00:21:55.640 --> 00:22:01.970
to the right and that I see among many young
people a return to traditional religion as

00:22:01.970 --> 00:22:05.440
a kind of — a hunger for spirituality that
is missing.

00:22:05.440 --> 00:22:06.440
Ben Wattenberg: John, do you see that going
on?

00:22:06.440 --> 00:22:07.440
John Leo: Oh, I think so, too.

00:22:07.440 --> 00:22:11.340
I think, just to pick up the thought about
liberalism, that liberalism has never really

00:22:11.340 --> 00:22:13.640
been endorsed by any major religion.

00:22:13.640 --> 00:22:16.950
Everybody feels a little uncomfortable about
the emptiness of it.

00:22:16.950 --> 00:22:25.140
And I think there is the beginning of a turn
toward a religious — but I don’t see any

00:22:25.140 --> 00:22:26.800
religion filling that need.

00:22:26.800 --> 00:22:30.980
You get books like Gertrude Himmelfarb calling
for a return to Victorian values.

00:22:30.980 --> 00:22:32.360
Well, who’s going to do that?

00:22:32.360 --> 00:22:36.840
Well, normally a church does something like
that, but no church now has that status quite,

00:22:36.840 --> 00:22:37.870
or that grasp of the American imagination.

00:22:37.870 --> 00:22:42.570
Ben Wattenberg: All right, let’s have just
one final little conversation here.

00:22:42.570 --> 00:22:44.140
Does this belong in politics?

00:22:44.140 --> 00:22:46.710
Are these political issues?

00:22:46.710 --> 00:22:51.160
Bob Dole was criticized, in part, for saying
it: “Oh, he’s never really paid much attention

00:22:51.160 --> 00:22:53.840
to this and now he’s making a big political
issue out of it.”

00:22:53.840 --> 00:22:57.270
President Clinton is apparently telling some
of his staff aides, “How come he’s getting

00:22:57.270 --> 00:22:58.270
all the publicity?

00:22:58.270 --> 00:23:01.120
I mentioned it first.”

00:23:01.120 --> 00:23:02.120
(Laughter.)

00:23:02.120 --> 00:23:03.210
Should this be part of this political debate?

00:23:03.210 --> 00:23:04.680
Robert Bork: Certainly it should.

00:23:04.680 --> 00:23:08.070
A political leader is not just somebody who
talks about economics.

00:23:08.070 --> 00:23:10.380
He’s also a cultural force.

00:23:10.380 --> 00:23:13.390
And there’s no reason why that cultural
force shouldn’t be exerted in good directions.

00:23:13.390 --> 00:23:16.260
Camille Paglia: Well, I feel, look at the
Kennedys.

00:23:16.260 --> 00:23:20.260
I mean, Mrs. Kennedy brought a distinction
and a kind of feeling for high art to the

00:23:20.260 --> 00:23:22.300
White House that I think we sorely need.

00:23:22.300 --> 00:23:24.760
John Leo: I think politicians should speak
out.

00:23:24.760 --> 00:23:27.500
I just don’t want Washington to do anything
about it.

00:23:27.500 --> 00:23:29.260
I think they’ll only screw it up.

00:23:29.260 --> 00:23:34.500
I think it belongs to the people, that we’re
trying to grapple with a highly democratic,

00:23:34.500 --> 00:23:38.810
wild and raucous public culture and we have
to grapple with it ourselves.

00:23:38.810 --> 00:23:43.240
Nobody is going to impose an answer to this,
and Bob Dole and President Clinton are not

00:23:43.240 --> 00:23:44.240
going to solve our problems.

00:23:44.240 --> 00:23:47.890
I’m glad they’re talking about it, Bill
Bradley, too, but I don’t want them to solve

00:23:47.890 --> 00:23:48.890
it for us.

00:23:48.890 --> 00:23:49.890
They’ll only mess it up.

00:23:49.890 --> 00:23:53.350
Ben Wattenberg: Do you think in this wild
and raucous culture there is also some very

00:23:53.350 --> 00:23:54.350
vibrant health?

00:23:54.350 --> 00:23:55.350
I mean, I think so.

00:23:55.350 --> 00:23:56.350
Does anybody here —

00:23:56.350 --> 00:23:57.350
Camille Paglia: Oh, yes.

00:23:57.350 --> 00:23:58.350
Oh, yes, I love America.

00:23:58.350 --> 00:24:00.270
I think America is the healthiest place in
the world.

00:24:00.270 --> 00:24:03.340
Ben Wattenberg: You are “slouching toward
Gomorrah” in your book.

00:24:03.340 --> 00:24:07.210
Robert Bork: But I think that’s true of
Western civilization in general.

00:24:07.210 --> 00:24:10.640
The industrialized countries of Western Europe
are sort of heading in the same direction

00:24:10.640 --> 00:24:14.820
we are, and the poor Arab world, the Muslim
world, they’re trying to keep it out.

00:24:14.820 --> 00:24:18.780
They’ve got the Iranian police running around
looking for satellite dishes so they can’t

00:24:18.780 --> 00:24:20.390
get American culture.

00:24:20.390 --> 00:24:25.640
It’s ridiculous; it’s dictatorial, but
one does see that they have a certain kind

00:24:25.640 --> 00:24:26.640
of point.

00:24:26.640 --> 00:24:29.330
Ben Wattenberg: You’re anti-censorship in
Iran, but in favor of it here?

00:24:29.330 --> 00:24:30.300
No. John —

00:24:30.300 --> 00:24:33.220
John Leo: I think the good part about this
cultural moment is that everything is now

00:24:33.230 --> 00:24:34.230
on the table.

00:24:34.230 --> 00:24:37.030
Everyone has a voice; everyone’s a publisher;
everyone’s a consumer.

00:24:37.030 --> 00:24:39.330
There’s no suppressed minority.

00:24:39.330 --> 00:24:41.110
Everybody can say whatever they want.

00:24:41.110 --> 00:24:45.410
And you’re getting a lot of pain now, but
I think something good could come out of it.

00:24:45.410 --> 00:24:46.760
Ben Wattenberg: OK.

00:24:46.760 --> 00:24:52.100
Thank you, Camille Paglia, Robert Bork, and
John Leo.

00:24:52.100 --> 00:24:53.130
And thank you.

